            <div xml:id="d751">

                <pb facs="0659" n="650" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg650"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/47<hi rendition="#superscript"
                        >7/22</hi>: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hong Kong</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Southard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hong Kong</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-06-30T17:00:00+08:00">June 30, 1940—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 30—7:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>220. Reference Department’s 103, June 28, 7 p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">6
                        p.m.</hi>]. Governor of Hong Kong has today telegraphed through British
                    Consul General at Saigon to Governor General of Indochina his approval of
                    shipment of stocks of wolfram and antimony to the United States.</p>
                <p>I am telegraphing Reed at Hanoi to get in touch with Bell of the Pekin Syndicate
                    who is handling shipping arrangements at Haiphong as the Governor here has
                    requested me to do so.</p>
                <p>Sent to Department.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Southard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
